tlantic on a wind-powered cargo ship</summary><link href="https://www.theatlantic.com/magazine/2027/01/neoliner-origin-wind-powered-cargo-ship/689402/?utm_source=feed" rel="alternate" type="text/html"></link></entry><entry><id>tag:theatlantic.com,2026:50-689530</id><author><name>Joshua Partlow</name><uri>http://www.theatlantic.com/author/joshua-partlow/?utm_source=feed</uri></author><media:content url="https://cdn.theatlantic.com/thumbor/YZbUMPP_c7NnhVd3vjlbZAw0h58=/media/img/mt/2026/11/2026_11_20_Climate_Action_Gets_Practical_Josh_Partlow/original.jpg"><media:credit>Metin Aktas / Anadolu / Getty</media:credit></media:content><title type="html">The World’s Biggest Climate Conference Just Fell Apart</title><published>2026-11-22T10:47:38-05:00</published><updated>2026-11-23T08:58:26-05:00</updated><summary type="html">Delegates at the annual UN climate summit failed to reach an agreement for the first time in almost 20 years.</summary><link href="https://www.theatlantic.com/science/2026/11/cop31/689530/?utm_source=feed" rel="alternate" type="text/html"></link></entry><entry><id>tag:theatlantic.com,2026:50-689481</id><author><name>Adam Frank</name><uri>http://www.theatlantic.com/author/adam-frank/?utm_source=feed</uri></author><media:content url="https://cdn.theatlantic.com/thumbor/CLsYjxwWlNnM101qC9ggyK6qNfc=/media/img/mt/2026/11/2026_11_18_Voyager_1/original.jpg"><media:credit>Illustration by The Atlantic *</media:credit></media:content><title type="html">A New Milestone for Life on Earth</title><published>2026-11-18T05:16:00-05:00</published><updated>2026-11-18T12:30:59-05:00</updated><summary type="html">After billions of years of taking from the universe, our planet has sent a piece of itself one light-day away.</summary><link href="https://www.theatlantic.com/science/2026/11/voyager-light-day-life-earth/689481/?utm_source=feed" rel="alternate" type="text/html"></link></entry><entry><id>tag:theatlantic.com,2026:50-689418</id><author><name>Benjamin Siegel</name><uri>http://www.theatlantic.com/author/benjamin-siegel/?utm_source=feed</uri></author><media:content url="https://cdn.theatlantic.com/thumbor/F2NeWMmmtKA9NFh9RIQRONhD564=/media/img/mt/2026/11/2026_11_17_The_Salmon_Crunch_is_Coming_Benjamin_Siegel/original.jpg"><media:credit>Anna Breit / Connected Archives</media:credit></media:content><title type="html">Is This the End of Cheap Salmon?</title><published>2026-11-17T09:31:38-05:00</published><updated>2026-11-17T15:51:23-05:00</updated><summary type="html">More people than ever want to eat the fish, but salmon farmers are closing in on their limits.</summary><link href="https://www.theatlantic.com/science/2026/11/salmon-farming/689418/?utm_source=feed" rel="alternate" type="text/html"></link></entry></feed>